What you may do

1. Download, install and run official releases of itsloopyo's software that
   include cameraunlock-core, such as Headstart and itsloopyo's head tracking
   mods, free of charge. Official releases are the ones itsloopyo publishes on the
   Releases page of each project's GitHub repository under
   https://github.com/itsloopyo, on the mod sites itsloopyo publishes them to,
   and the updates that software downloads through its own updater.

2. Read and change the source code, and build and run it on computers you
   control for your own use, including CI runs for your own fork, as long as
   nobody else is given the builds and they are not published as artifacts or
   releases.

3. Publish your changes on GitHub, in a fork of this repository, to offer them
   to itsloopyo as a pull request under the agreement in CONTRIBUTING.md at
   https://github.com/itsloopyo/cameraunlock-core. A
   fork may hold nothing else, and builds of it may not be distributed.

What you may not do without itsloopyo's written permission

- Distribute cameraunlock-core, a build of it, or a changed version of it, in
  whole or in part, alone or inside other software.
- Use any part of cameraunlock-core's source code in other software.

GitHub's terms of service let GitHub users view and fork this repository on
GitHub. That right goes no further: a fork may be built only as point 2 allows,
and neither the fork nor builds of it may be distributed or used in other
software, except as point 3 allows.

If you break these terms, the permissions above end for you.

What this licence does not cover

- The third-party code in the repository's vendor folder, and the third-party
  components listed in its legal/README.md (all but cameraunlock-core itself),
  are under their own licences, given there. Nothing in this licence limits
  your rights under them. Where a file of cameraunlock-core is ported from or
  follows one of those projects, that project's part stays under its own
  licence.

- Files cameraunlock-core writes into itsloopyo's mod repositories (install
  and uninstall scripts, build tasks, workflow settings and the like) are
  under the licence of the repository they are written into.

